First Answer (30-second Version)

If the interviewer asks this, you should answer like this:

"String is immutable to improve security, enable efficient memory usage through the String Pool, make it inherently thread-safe, and ensure reliable behavior when used as keys in collections like HashMap."

This is a complete high-level answer.

The interviewer will almost always ask "Can you explain each point?"

Then you go deeper.

1. Security (Most Important)

Suppose you're connecting to a database.

String password = "admin123";

Imagine if String were mutable.

Some other method could do:

password.replace(...);

Now your password has changed without your knowledge.

The same happens with

Database passwords
JWT Tokens
API Keys
Bank Account Numbers

Immutability prevents accidental or malicious modification.

2. String Pool (Performance)

Java maintains a String Constant Pool.

Example:

String s1 = "Java";
String s2 = "Java";

Both point to the same object.

        String Pool

       +-----------+
s1 --->|  "Java"   |
s2 --->|           |
       +-----------+

If String were mutable,

s1 = s1.replace("J", "K");

Now should s2 also become "Kava"?

That would be incorrect.

Because String is immutable,
Java safely shares one object among many references.

This saves memory.

3. Thread Safety

Suppose:

Thread A

String name = "Saranya";

Thread B

uses the same String.

If Strings were mutable,

Thread A could change

Saranya

to

XYZ

while Thread B is reading.

Race condition.

Because Strings are immutable,

multiple threads can safely share the same String object.

No synchronization required.

4. HashMap Performance

Suppose

Map<String,Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

map.put("Account123",1000);

HashMap calculates

hashCode()

using the String.

Now imagine String is mutable.

Account123

changes to

Account999

Its hashCode changes.

Now HashMap cannot find the key anymore.

That breaks the HashMap contract.

Immutability guarantees

hashCode()

never changes.

5. Caching hashCode()

Since String never changes,

Java calculates hashCode once.

Then stores it.

Next time

hashCode()

is called,

Java returns the cached value.

Very fast.

If String were mutable,

Java would need to calculate it every time.

Interviewer Counter Question

Q: If String is immutable, how does this work?

String s = "Java";

s = s.concat("21");

Expected answer:

concat() does not modify the existing String. It creates a new String object "Java21" and then the reference s is updated to point to the new object. The original "Java" object remains unchanged.

